WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:04.620
Hello everyone, welcome to the Langfocus Channel, and my name is Paul.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.660
Today we'll be looking at why Greek sounds like Spanish.

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:11.020
What? Greek Sounds like Spanish?!

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:15.740
If you've never noticed it yourself, than I'm sure that idea sounds ridiculous.

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:20.560
I mean, they're both Indo-European languages and share a common ancestor language,

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.280
but Greek essentially forms its own branch of Indo-European,

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.160
while Spanish is a member of the Romance language branch.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:30.960
But many native speakers of both languages say that they sound alike.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:37.260
For example, some Spanish speakers say that Greek sounds like a Spanish speaker talking in a made-up language

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:40.040
without even trying to fake a foreign accent.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.680
But do they really sound alike?

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:46.060
Before we go any further, let's stop for a minute and hear a sample of each language.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:49.760
Listen with an open mind, and see if you think they sound alike.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.760
First, European Spanish.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:59.920
[Spanish]  'Barcelona is located on the Mediterranean coast. It is famous for its modernist architecture, its mild climate, and its open-minded people.'

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:06.080
'The city is bordered by the sea to the east, and by mountains to the west, and it has about 4,000 years of history.'

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:15.440
'Its primary attraction is the Sagrada Familia, a Catholic basilica designed by Antoni Gaudí, that is still in construction 136 years after the work began.'

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.520
And now, Modern Greek.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:51.200
[Greek] 'Athens is my city. It is Greece' capital. Located in Attica prefecture, near the Aegean sea, Athens is one of the world's oldest cities. Its main monument being the Parthenon, the most attractive places here are Plaka, Thiseio, and Monastiraki. The majority of Greeks live and work in the capital. Next to Athens can be found many towns and islands, that its residents can visit for a getaway.'

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.400
So, what do you think? Do they sound alike?

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.400
I'm willing to bet that a lot of you said "yes".

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:58.000
So what makes them sound similar?

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.280
In many ways, they have similar phonology.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:05.960
This is particularly true when we're comparing Modern Greek to European Spanish, or "Iberian" Spanish.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:07.480
Similar consonants.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:10.600
If we ignore writing and focus only on sounds,

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.140
the Spanish and Greek consonant inventories —

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.880
— that's just a fancy way of saying "the consonants that appear in each language" —

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:18.880
— are almost exactly the same.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:23.360
I imagine that a lot of you are more familiar with Spanish consonants than Greek ones,

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:28.340
but Greek also shares most of the consonants that you might consider "characteristically Spanish".

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:31.360
Xa. Here is a Spanish example: (Spanish speaking)

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.860
Here is a Greek example: (Greek speaking)

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.600
ʎa... (Spanish speaking)

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:38.320
(Greek speaking)

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:42.300
ɤa... This represents the soft G sound in Spanish.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.060
For example: (Spanish speaking), meaning "giant".

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:49.700
Here, the second G is pronounced as a soft G: ɤ (reiterated Spanish speaking)

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:53.000
And in this Greek word, meaning "blue" (Greek speaking)

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:57.340
In Greek, this letter is always pronounced with that soft G sound.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:01.800
θa... a Spanish example: (Spanish speaking), meaning "city",

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:05.220
and a Greek example: (Greek speaking), meaning "anger".

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.300
The "θ" sound doesn't appear in most European languages,

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:12.620
so it may stand out as quite a noticeable characteristic of Spanish and Greek

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:14.360
and make them sound somewhat similar.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:19.800
Though, it's worth noting that this "θ" sound is mainly a feature of Iberian Spanish, "European" Spanish,

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:22.820
and doesn't appear in other dialects of Spanish.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:27.000
ɲa... a Spanish example: (Spanish speaking), meaning "bathroom".

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:30.400
In Standard Greek this isn't really a separate consonant,

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:35.320
but basically, the same sound can occur if "n" is followed by certain diphthongs,

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:37.700
and it's also used in some Greek dialects.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.540
In Greek: (Greek speaking), also meaning "restroom".

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:43.680
But wait a minute. Why is that word the same in both languages?

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.720
Well, we'll get back to that a little later.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.740
And then there are rhotic sounds.

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:52.160
Rhotic sounds (or "R" sounds) are partly similar and partly different.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:54.220
Spanish has two R sounds:

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:56.380
an alveolar tap: ɾa

00:03:56.380 --> 00:03:58.880
...and an alveolar trill: Rrrrrrrrrr

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:01.280
(Greek speaking), meaning "stop".

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.720
This rhotic sound is an alveolar tap.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:04.460
(Greek enunciating "ɾ")

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:08.500
In Greek, trills occur occasionally in certain positions, but they're short.

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:11.260
The main sound that occurs is the alveolar tap,

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:14.680
similar to the Spanish one, just a "ɾ" sound.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:19.460
And between vowels, it can sometimes also be an alveolar approximant: "r"

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:22.040
like the R sound at the beginning of words in English,

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:26.240
but the most common realization of the Greek rhotic is as a tap,

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:27.420
as it is in Spanish.

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:29.620
(Spanish speaking), meaning "window".

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:31.620
This is also an alveolar tap.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:33.900
(Greek speaking), (reiterated Spanish speaking)

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:35.780
Similar vowels.

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:40.500
When it comes to the vowels of Spanish and Greek, the similarity is clear as a bell.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.660
Both languages have the same set of five vowels:

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:44.160
(Spanish enunciating "a")

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:44.980
(Greek enunciating "a")

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:45.700
(Spanish enunciating "e")

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:46.480
(Greek enunciating "e")

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:47.140
(Spanish enunciating "i")

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:48.140
(Greek enunciating "i")

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:48.720
(Spanish enunciating "o")

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:49.500
(Greek enunciating "o")

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:50.220
(Spanish enunciating "u")

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:51.000
(Greek enunciating "u")

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:56.700
Also neither language has any phonemic distinction between short vowels and long vowels.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:00.560
In Greek, there are more than five symbols to indicate these vowels

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:03.520
because some symbols represent identical sounds.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:06.640
And the vowel "u" is represented by a digraph.

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:09.820
But in terms of phonetics, there are just those five vowels.

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:14.840
Now let's have a look at a couple of words from each of the short sample speeches we heard before.

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:18.120
From the Spanish sample: (Spanish speaking)

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:20.660
This means "modernist architecture",

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:24.560
and we can see each of the five vowels in this phrase, and no others.

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:26.100
(reiterated Spanish speaking)

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:29.700
And a phrase from the Greek sample: (Greek speaking)

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:31.820
This means "the capital city".

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:34.900
Again, each of the five vowels are present, and no others.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:36.800
(reiterated Greek speaking)

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:39.160
Both languages contain some diphthongs,

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:45.840
but since diphthongs are combinations of the five basic vowels, just with two of them side by side in one syllable,

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:47.720
many of the diphthongs are the same.

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:50.560
Spanish has (Spanish enunciating "ai/ay")

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:52.360
For example: (Spanish speaking),

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:54.180
meaning "there is" or "there are".

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:58.240
With its silent H, this word just sounds like the diphthong itself.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:00.320
And Greek has (Greek enunciating "ai")

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:02.540
For example: (Greek speaking),

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:03.700
meaning "donkey".

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:05.840
Spanish has (Spanish enunciating "oi/oy")

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:07.060
(Spanish speaking),

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:08.440
meaning "today".

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:11.000
And Greek has (Greek enunciating "oi")

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:11.820
(Greek speaking),

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:13.020
meaning "sucker".

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:17.680
Spanish also has several diphthongs that begin with the sound "i"

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:21.020
(Spanish enunciating "ia, ie, io, iu")

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:22.860
For example: (Spanish speaking),

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:24.020
meaning "city".

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:29.980
And in Greek, there are similar diphthongs that begin with E and are followed by a vowel with an accent.

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:31.920
For example: (Spanish speaking),

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:33.100
meaning "children".

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:39.400
And in Spanish, there are some other diphthongs that are not common in Modern Greek and mostly appear in loanwords,

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:42.100
so the diphthongs are not entirely the same.

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:43.700
Syllable structure.

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:47.340
In terms of syllable structure, Spanish and Greek are fairly similar.

00:06:47.349 --> 00:06:53.579
Both languages contain a lot of open syllables, meaning that the syllables end in a vowel rather than a consonant.

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:58.000
For example: the Spanish word: (Spanish speaking), meaning "van",

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:01.580
and a Greek word: (Greek speaking), meaning "I understand".

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:07.340
You might notice that these words end in vowels, which of course, means that their final syllables end in vowels.

00:07:07.420 --> 00:07:09.760
The other syllables end in vowels too.

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:14.340
The prominence of these open syllables gives a certain sound and rhythm to the languages.

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:21.600
Both languages have closed syllables as well, but in both languages, only a limited number of consonants can appear in the coda.

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:25.820
In other words, the end of a closed syllable, especially at the end of a word.

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:29.800
In both languages, the final consonant of the word, if there is a final consonant,

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:33.700
can be "n" or "s", and in Greek that's it.

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:39.640
In Spanish, a few other sounds are possible, including the rhotic "l", "d", and "θ".

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:44.040
But the most common seem to be "n", "s", and the rhotic sound.

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:48.300
In Greek, (Greek speaking),

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:53.860
and in Spanish, (Spanish speaking)

00:07:55.060 --> 00:08:00.520
In Spanish, one of the reasons the final "s" sound is common is that it indicates the plural,

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:06.140
and since masculine nouns commonly end in "o", and feminine nouns commonly end in "a",

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:11.540
many plural words end in "os" in the masculine or "as" in the feminine.

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:13.940
(Spanish speaking)

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:19.100
There are two similar endings in Greek, not for the plural, but they indicate the nominative case.

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:24.760
But hearing a lot of "os" and "as" at the end of words might add to the similar sound of the languages.

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:27.180
(Greek speaking)

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:33.760
You may have noticed that in both the Spanish speakers and Greek speakers, pronunciation the S sound is a little different from mine,

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:35.440
especially after "o".

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:39.560
That's because in Greek and European Spanish, at least in Castilian Spanish,

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:45.240
the S sound is retracted, pronounced a little further back on the roof of the mouth than the English "s".

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:49.220
So after "o" it may sound like something between "s" and "sh".

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:50.220
(Spanish speaking)

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:51.460
(Greek speaking)

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:57.980
On top of that, there are noticeable similarities between Spanish and Greek verb conjugations.

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:02.520
Let's take a look at the present tense forms of the verbs meaning "make" or "do".

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:05.580
"I do"... (Spanish speaking)... (Greek speaking)

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:08.860
"You do" singular... (Spanish speaking)... (Greek speaking)

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:12.120
"He does" or "she does"... (Spanish speaking)... (Greek speaking)

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:15.460
"We do"... (Spanish speaking)... (Greek speaking)

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:18.980
"You do" plural... (Spanish speaking)... (Greek speaking)

00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:22.080
"They do"... (Spanish speaking)... (Greek speaking)

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:25.680
I'm sure you would agree that there's some similarity in these conjugations,

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:31.040
and since endings like these appear so often, they have an effect on the overall sound of each language.

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:33.300
Stress and syllable timing.

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:36.440
In the video, "Why Does Portuguese Sound Like Russian?",

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:40.280
I said that both Portuguese and Russian are stress-timed languages,

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:45.900
meaning that stressed syllables become longer, and unstressed vowels undergo a lot of reduction.

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:48.760
Well, there are also syllable-timed languages,

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:53.980
in which each syllable is more or less the same length and there isn't a lot of vowel reduction.

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:59.820
Spanish is a syllable-timed language, and Greek is sometimes considered a syllable-timed language,

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:06.280
but it's often left unclassified, or is thought to be somewhere in the middle between stress-timed and syllable-timed.

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:09.700
It's not heavily stress-timed like Portuguese or Russian,

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:16.720
but stressed syllables are somewhat longer, and there is some vowel reduction that affects the quality of the vowel, but not too much.

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:23.120
If we look at this graph here, we can see that Greek vowels vary in length somewhat more than Spanish vowels,

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:28.580
but Greek is in the middle, between Spanish and languages that are normally considered stress-timed.

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:33.480
But other direction at the bottom represents the variability of time between vowels,

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:36.700
and Greek and Spanish are almost the same in this regard.

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:44.920
That similarity, along with the lack of strong vowel reduction in either language, might contribute to the perception of their similar sound and rhythm.

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:46.600
Vocabulary.

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:52.900
So, we've looked at the phonological similarities of Spanish and Greek, but what about the similarities in vocabulary?

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:57.480
Well, I think this is not as important to the way the languages sound as phonology

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:02.440
because a lot of the people who think they sound alike don't really know many words in either language.

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:06.000
They're judging the similarity just based on how the languages sound.

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:09.780
But some people may also recognize some similar words in either language.

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:14.680
Spanish developed from Latin, and Latin adopted some vocabulary of Greek origin.

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:20.020
These are some Spanish words that ultimately came from Greek, alongside their Modern Greek equivalent

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:35.460
(Spanish/Greek speakers alternating enunciation)

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:42.740
And there are also some Latin-derived words in Greek, some of them from Italian or regional languages of Italy.

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:50.320
So to the extent that Spanish and Italian sound similar, those Greek words might sounds Spanish-y, or at least Romance-based.

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:54.080
For example: (Greek speaking), from Latin "porta".

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:56.000
And in Spanish: (Spanish speaking)

00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:59.160
(Greek speaking), from Italian "bagno".

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:01.160
And in Spanish: (Spanish speaking)

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:04.740
(Greek speaking), from Venetian "cusina".

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:06.960
And in Spanish: (Spanish speaking)

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:12.400
But shared cognate vocabulary is certainly nothing unique among these two languages.

00:12:12.580 --> 00:12:18.980
There are lots of words of Greek origin and lots of words of Latin origin in all of the Indo-European languages of Europe.

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:23.140
The presence of those cognate words doesn't make those languages sound similar,

00:12:23.140 --> 00:12:26.300
but the sounds of those words make the languages sound similar.

00:12:26.300 --> 00:12:32.620
That includes the open syllables and the final vowels. That includes the similar vowel sounds and the similar consonants.

00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:36.900
In other words the phonology of the two languages is what makes them sound similar.

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:38.400
The question of the day!

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:44.140
After hearing the spoken samples of Spanish and Greek side-by-side in the video, do you think they sound similar?

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:45.940
What similarities do you notice?

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:51.240
And for native speakers of either language, have you ever thought that Spanish and Greek sound similar?

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:53.260
What do you think gave you that impression?

